  title: "Game loop: print each fixed tick",
  minutes: 12,
  unit: "Loop",
  explanation: "A game does not draw once and exit. It repeats a tick: sample the world, update it, then present it. This lesson fixes the number of ticks from the input so the run stays short enough to grade. A shipped loop would continue until the player quits. The body of the tick is the part that will grow when you add movement. The header is the part you practice here, and it already has a rule for doing nothing.\n\nfor (int tick = 1; tick <= n; ++tick) is that header. The initializer runs once. The condition is tested before every pass, so an n of 0 never enters and a negative n never enters either. The increment runs after the body. Declaring tick in the header keeps the name local to the loop. Later code cannot trip over a leftover counter, and a second loop can reuse the idea without sharing the variable.\n\nRead one int and default it to 0 before the extraction. Print tick 1, then tick 2, and so on, one line at a time, through n inclusive. An n of 3 prints three lines. An n of 1 prints a single line. An n of 0 prints nothing. A missing stream leaves the default, so it also prints nothing. There is no title line and no total after the last tick.\n\nThe starter prints tick 0, which this protocol never produces for an accepted count. Replace that line with the loop. Insert one line per tick with std::cout. Stop when the counter passes n. Do not wait for a sentinel value in the input. The count is the whole protocol, and the word tick is lowercase.",
  sample: "for (int tick = 1; tick <= n; ++tick) std::cout << \"tick \" << tick << \"\\n\";",
