  explanation: "A sprite's next position is its current position plus its velocity. Velocity here is an int, in cells per tick, and one tick is the whole program. You are not drawing the path. You are applying the step that a game loop would apply once before it drew. Positive velocity walks to the right. Negative velocity walks to the left. Zero velocity leaves the sprite where it stands.\n\nRead two ints, x and vx, and default both to 0 before the extraction. Add vx to x. Print the new x on its own line. There is no label and no second number. 3 and 2 print 5. 4 and -1 print 3. 0 and 0 print 0. A missing stream leaves both defaults, so the line is 0. A missing second number leaves vx at 0, so the line is the x you did read.\n\nThe addition is an int addition. Do not call a rounding helper and do not promote the values to floating point. A later lesson repeats this step inside a counted loop. If the single step is wrong, the path will drift by that error on every tick. Keep the update in one place: x += vx, then print x.\n\nThe starter prints 0 and does not read. That happens to match the empty stream and the pair 0 0, and it fails every real step, including a negative velocity. Read both numbers, apply the velocity, and print the result. Use std::cin and std::cout.",
