  unit: "Loop",
  explanation: "Integration is the velocity step repeated. Each tick adds the velocity to the position, and the position you print is the one after that addition, not the one from the start of the tick. Doing this for a fixed count is how a game predicts a straight path without storing an array of points. The velocity stays constant in this lesson. Acceleration can wait.\n\nRead x, vx, and n, and default all three to 0. If n is negative, treat it as no ticks. For each tick from 1 through n, add vx to x and print x on its own line. 0, 2, and 3 print 2, then 4, then 6. 5, -1, and 2 print 4, then 3. Zero ticks print nothing, and the starting x is not a line by itself. A missing stream prints nothing, because n stays 0. Do not print the velocity, the tick index, or a final summary.\n\nThe loop body is two statements: the update, then the insertion. If you print before you add, every line is one step behind and the first line is the start position, which these tests do not want. If you add n times and print once, you have the destination and you have lost the path. The path is the assignment.\n\nThe starter prints 0. That is not the empty output of a zero-tick run, and it is not the path of a real velocity. Read the three ints and print once per tick, after the update. std::cout inserts the int and a newline.",
